/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · 2 Chronicles chapters 26-30
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("2chronicles", [
{n:26, name:"Uzziah's Leprosy", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 26:16",verse:"But when he was strong, his heart was lifted up to his destruction: for he transgressed against the LORD his God, and went into the temple of the LORD to burn incense upon the altar of incense."}, facts:["Did you know Uzziah's engineers invented machines of war? Cunning men in Jerusalem built engines to shoot arrows and hurl great stones from the towers and battlements.","Did you know Uzziah commanded 307,500 fighting men, organized under 2,600 chiefs of the fathers, yet his great strength became his downfall?","Did you know Uzziah spent his final years as a leper in a separate house, cut off from the temple, while his son Jotham governed the people in his place?"], questions:[
{q:"How old was Uzziah when the people of Judah made him king in place of his father Amaziah?", options:["16 years old","12 years old","20 years old","25 years old"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:1",verse:"Then all the people of Judah took Uzziah, who was sixteen years old, and made him king in the room of his father Amaziah.", insight:"Uzziah's reign began young, which makes his long years of faithfulness all the more striking. God can use a teenager whose heart is set to seek Him."},
{q:"How long did Uzziah reign in Jerusalem?", options:["52 years","40 years","29 years","16 years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:3",verse:"Sixteen years old was Uzziah when he began to reign, and he reigned fifty and two years in Jerusalem. His mother’s name also was Jecoliah of Jerusalem.", insight:"Fifty two years of rule is a long stewardship. Length of reign is a gift, but only a humble heart keeps the gift from turning into pride."},
{q:"In whose days did Uzziah seek God, so that God made him prosper?", options:["Zechariah, who had understanding in the visions of God","Nathan the prophet","Gad the seer","Isaiah the son of Amoz"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:5",verse:"And he sought God in the days of Zechariah, who had understanding in the visions of God: and as long as he sought the LORD, God made him to prosper.", insight:"Uzziah prospered as long as he sought God, and the verse ties the two together directly. Seeking God is not decoration on success, it is the source of it."},
{q:"Which Philistine cities had their walls broken down by Uzziah?", options:["Gath, Jabneh, and Ashdod","Gaza, Ekron, and Ashkelon","Gath and Gaza only","Ashdod and Ekron"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:6",verse:"And he went forth and warred against the Philistines, and brake down the wall of Gath, and the wall of Jabneh, and the wall of Ashdod, and built cities about Ashdod, and among the Philistines.", insight:"God gave Uzziah victory over entrenched enemies and he built cities in their territory. Real strength shows in what you build after the battle, not only in what you break."},
{q:"Which people gave gifts to Uzziah, so that his name spread abroad even to Egypt?", options:["The Ammonites","The Moabites","The Edomites","The Syrians"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:8",verse:"And the Ammonites gave gifts to Uzziah: and his name spread abroad even to the entering in of Egypt; for he strengthened himself exceedingly.", insight:"Uzziah's fame reached Egypt because God strengthened him. A good name is a trust from God, not a trophy to admire."},
{q:"Where in Jerusalem did Uzziah build towers and fortify them?", options:["At the corner gate, the valley gate, and the turning of the wall","At the sheep gate, the fish gate, and the old gate","Around the temple courts only","At the water gate and the horse gate"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:9",verse:"Moreover Uzziah built towers in Jerusalem at the corner gate, and at the valley gate, and at the turning of the wall, and fortified them.", insight:"Uzziah strengthened the weak points of the city wall, the gates and the turns. Wisdom defends the places where attack is most likely, not only the places that look impressive."},
{q:"What did Uzziah build in the desert, and what did he dig there?", options:["Towers, and many wells for his much cattle","A palace and a garden","A fortress and a granary","Watchtowers and cisterns for wine"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:10",verse:"Also he built towers in the desert, and digged many wells: for he had much cattle, both in the low country, and in the plains: husbandmen also, and vine dressers in the mountains, and in Carmel: for he loved husbandry.", insight:"Uzziah cared for the quiet work of wells and cattle as well as the glory of war. Faithfulness in unglamorous provision is part of good leadership too."},
{q:"Why did Uzziah employ many husbandmen and vinedressers in the mountains and in Carmel?", options:["For he loved husbandry","To feed his standing army","To pay tribute to Assyria","To trade produce with Egypt"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:10",verse:"Also he built towers in the desert, and digged many wells: for he had much cattle, both in the low country, and in the plains: husbandmen also, and vine dressers in the mountains, and in Carmel: for he loved husbandry.", insight:"The king loved the soil and the work of growing things. Loving honest labor keeps a powerful man grounded in reality."},
{q:"How many men of war served under the 2,600 chiefs of the fathers in Uzziah's host?", options:["307,500","120,000","200,000","580,000"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:13",verse:"And under their hand was an army, three hundred thousand and seven thousand and five hundred, that made war with mighty power, to help the king against the enemy.", insight:"An army of over three hundred thousand marched under organized leadership. Great resources demand great humility, because they whisper that you no longer need God."},
{q:"What armor and weapons did Uzziah prepare for the whole host?", options:["Shields, spears, helmets, habergeons, bows, and slings","Swords and chariots only","Swords, shields, and battering rams","Bows, arrows, and iron helmets only"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:14",verse:"And Uzziah prepared for them throughout all the host shields, and spears, and helmets, and habergeons, and bows, and slings to cast stones.", insight:"Uzziah left nothing unequipped; every soldier had what he needed. A leader's care shows in the details of preparation, not only in the speeches before battle."},
{q:"What did the cunning men in Jerusalem invent to set upon the towers and battlements?", options:["Engines to shoot arrows and cast great stones","Iron chariots","Battering rams","Bronze gates"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:15",verse:"And he made in Jerusalem engines, invented by cunning men, to be on the towers and upon the bulwarks, to shoot arrows and great stones withal. And his name spread far abroad; for he was marvellously helped, till he was strong.", insight:"God gave men skill to invent new defenses, and the verse says Uzziah was marvellously helped till he was strong. Gifts of skill are meant to be received with thanks, not turned into self worship."},
{q:"What did Uzziah do when his heart was lifted up to his destruction?", options:["He went into the temple of the LORD to burn incense upon the altar of incense","He offered sacrifices on the high places","He built an idol in the temple courts","He stopped the daily sacrifices"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:16",verse:"But when he was strong, his heart was lifted up to his destruction: for he transgressed against the LORD his God, and went into the temple of the LORD to burn incense upon the altar of incense.", insight:"Uzziah's fall began the moment he reached for a priest's office that was not his. Pride does not always look like rebellion; sometimes it looks like serving God in a way He never asked for."},
{q:"Who followed Uzziah into the temple to confront him?", options:["Azariah the priest, with eighty priests, valiant men","The high priest alone","Jotham with the palace guard","Isaiah the prophet"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:17",verse:"And Azariah the priest went in after him, and with him fourscore priests of the LORD, that were valiant men:", insight:"Eighty one priests stood together against a powerful king. Courage multiplies when godly people refuse to watch sin in silence."},
{q:"What did the priests tell Uzziah when they withstood him?", options:["It is not for thee to burn incense, but for the priests, the sons of Aaron","Leave the temple and never return","Offer your incense in the outer court instead","Wait until the Sabbath to burn incense"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:18",verse:"And they withstood Uzziah the king, and said unto him, It appertaineth not unto thee, Uzziah, to burn incense unto the LORD, but to the priests the sons of Aaron, that are consecrated to burn incense: go out of the sanctuary; for thou hast trespassed; neither shall it be for thine honour from the LORD God.", insight:"The priests spoke plainly: this holy work belongs to those God appointed. Loving correction draws a line where God's holiness requires one."},
{q:"What happened to Uzziah while he was wroth, with the censer in his hand?", options:["Leprosy rose up in his forehead before the priests","His hand withered","Fire came out from the altar","The censer fell and broke"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:19",verse:"Then Uzziah was wroth, and had a censer in his hand to burn incense: and while he was wroth with the priests, the leprosy even rose up in his forehead before the priests in the house of the LORD, from beside the incense altar.", insight:"Judgment appeared on his forehead, visible to all, at the very moment of his anger. Sin confronted is a mercy; sin indulged writes itself where everyone can see."},
{q:"Why did Uzziah himself hasten to go out of the sanctuary?", options:["Because the LORD had smitten him","Because the priests thrust him out","Because the people cried out against him","Because the temple doors were shut"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:20",verse:"And Azariah the chief priest, and all the priests, looked upon him, and, behold, he was leprous in his forehead, and they thrust him out from thence; yea, himself hasted also to go out, because the LORD had smitten him.", insight:"Even Uzziah recognized the hand of the LORD in his affliction and hurried out. When God disciplines, the wise response is to yield quickly, not to argue."},
{q:"Where did Uzziah live after he became a leper, and what was he cut off from?", options:["In a separate house, cut off from the house of the LORD","In the palace, cut off from the army","In the wilderness, cut off from his family","In Egypt, cut off from Judah"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:21",verse:"And Uzziah the king was a leper unto the day of his death, and dwelt in a several house, being a leper; for he was cut off from the house of the LORD: and Jotham his son was over the king’s house, judging the people of the land.", insight:"The king who forced his way into the temple ended his days barred from it. The places we demand to enter in pride, we may lose entirely."},
{q:"Who judged the people of the land while Uzziah lived as a leper?", options:["Jotham his son","Azariah the priest","Isaiah the prophet","Hananiah the ruler"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:21",verse:"And Uzziah the king was a leper unto the day of his death, and dwelt in a several house, being a leper; for he was cut off from the house of the LORD: and Jotham his son was over the king’s house, judging the people of the land.", insight:"God's work did not stop because the king was set aside; Jotham governed in his place. The LORD's purposes are never held hostage by one man's failure."},
{q:"Who wrote the acts of Uzziah, first and last?", options:["Isaiah the prophet, the son of Amoz","Jeremiah the prophet","Nathan the prophet","The royal chronicler"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:22",verse:"Now the rest of the acts of Uzziah, first and last, did Isaiah the prophet, the son of Amoz, write.", insight:"Isaiah himself recorded Uzziah's story, triumph and tragedy together. A life written honestly becomes a warning and a teacher for generations."},
{q:"What did they say when they buried Uzziah with his fathers?", options:["He is a leper","He was a mighty builder","He sought God in his youth","He reigned fifty two years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 26:23",verse:"So Uzziah slept with his fathers, and they buried him with his fathers in the field of the burial which belonged to the kings; for they said, He is a leper: and Jotham his son reigned in his stead.", insight:"After all his victories, Uzziah was remembered for his leprosy. How a life ends can eclipse how it began, which is why finishing humble matters more than starting strong."}
]},
{n:27, name:"Jotham's Faithfulness", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 27:6",verse:"So Jotham became mighty, because he prepared his ways before the LORD his God."}, facts:["Did you know Jotham built the upper gate of the house of the LORD and fortified the wall of Ophel, yet unlike his father he never entered the temple to overstep his God given place?"], questions:[
{q:"How old was Jotham when he began to reign, and how long did he reign in Jerusalem?", options:["25 years old, and he reigned 16 years","16 years old, and he reigned 52 years","20 years old, and he reigned 16 years","25 years old, and he reigned 29 years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 27:1",verse:"Jotham was twenty and five years old when he began to reign, and he reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem. His mother’s name also was Jerushah, the daughter of Zadok.", insight:"Jotham began at twenty five and reigned sixteen years. A shorter reign can still be a faithful one; God measures obedience, not length of tenure."},
{q:"Who was Jotham's mother?", options:["Jerushah, the daughter of Zadok","Jecoliah of Jerusalem","Abijah, the daughter of Zechariah","Hephzibah"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 27:1",verse:"Jotham was twenty and five years old when he began to reign, and he reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem. His mother’s name also was Jerushah, the daughter of Zadok.", insight:"Jotham's mother was the daughter of Zadok, of priestly family. Godly heritage is a gift, and Jotham used it well instead of coasting on it."},
{q:"How did Jotham's conduct toward the temple differ from his father Uzziah's?", options:["He entered not into the temple of the LORD","He burned incense on the golden altar","He appointed priests of his own choosing","He offered sacrifices in the holy place"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 27:2",verse:"And he did that which was right in the sight of the LORD, according to all that his father Uzziah did: howbeit he entered not into the temple of the LORD. And the people did yet corruptly.", insight:"Jotham learned from his father's leprosy and stayed out of the priest's office. The wise learn from the falls of others instead of repeating them."},
{q:"Although Jotham did right, what did the people still do?", options:["They did corruptly","They followed him faithfully","They abandoned the high places","They returned to the law"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 27:2",verse:"And he did that which was right in the sight of the LORD, according to all that his father Uzziah did: howbeit he entered not into the temple of the LORD. And the people did yet corruptly.", insight:"A faithful king could not force a faithful people; they still did corruptly. Leaders can set the example, but each heart must choose for itself."},
{q:"What did Jotham build for the house of the LORD?", options:["The high gate","The porch","A new court","The altar of burnt offering"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 27:3",verse:"He built the high gate of the house of the LORD, and on the wall of Ophel he built much.", insight:"Jotham's first building project honored God's house with a high gate. Putting God's house first in our plans is a mark of a right ordered life."},
{q:"Where did Jotham build cities, and where did he build castles and towers?", options:["Cities in the mountains of Judah; castles and towers in the forests","Cities in the valley; towers in the desert","Cities by the sea; castles in Jerusalem","Cities in Ephraim; towers in the Negev"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 27:4",verse:"Moreover he built cities in the mountains of Judah, and in the forests he built castles and towers.", insight:"Jotham strengthened both the mountains and the forests, the settled places and the wild places. Diligence covers the whole of one's charge, not just the visible parts."},
{q:"Against whom did Jotham fight and prevail?", options:["The king of the Ammonites","The king of Moab","The king of Syria","The Philistines"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 27:5",verse:"He fought also with the king of the Ammonites, and prevailed against them. And the children of Ammon gave him the same year an hundred talents of silver, and ten thousand measures of wheat, and ten thousand of barley. So much did the children of Ammon pay unto him, both the second year, and the third.", insight:"Jotham prevailed against Ammon, the same people who once paid tribute to his father. Victories inherited still have to be won again by each generation."},
{q:"What yearly tribute did the children of Ammon pay to Jotham?", options:["100 talents of silver, 10,000 measures of wheat, and 10,000 of barley","1,000 talents of gold and 5,000 sheep","200 talents of silver and 20,000 measures of oil","100 talents of gold and 10,000 cattle"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 27:5",verse:"He fought also with the king of the Ammonites, and prevailed against them. And the children of Ammon gave him the same year an hundred talents of silver, and ten thousand measures of wheat, and ten thousand of barley. So much did the children of Ammon pay unto him, both the second year, and the third.", insight:"The tribute was measured and yearly, a steady fruit of steady strength. Consistent faithfulness produces consistent blessing."},
{q:"Why did Jotham become mighty?", options:["Because he prepared his ways before the LORD his God","Because he built strong towers","Because he hired mercenaries","Because he allied with Assyria"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 27:6",verse:"So Jotham became mighty, because he prepared his ways before the LORD his God.", insight:"Jotham's might came from prepared ways before God, not from weapons or alliances. Strength that is built in secret devotion holds up in public battle."},
{q:"Who reigned in Jotham's place when he slept with his fathers?", options:["Ahaz his son","Hezekiah his son","Uzziah his father","Pekah of Israel"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 27:9",verse:"And Jotham slept with his fathers, and they buried him in the city of David: and Ahaz his son reigned in his stead.", insight:"Faithful Jotham was followed by wicked Ahaz, his own son. Even the best parents cannot guarantee their children's choices, which makes prayer for them all the more urgent."}
]},
{n:28, name:"Ahaz's Apostasy", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 28:19",verse:"For the LORD brought Judah low because of Ahaz king of Israel; for he made Judah naked, and transgressed sore against the LORD."}, facts:["Did you know that in a single day Pekah of Israel slew 120,000 valiant men of Judah, because they had forsaken the LORD? It stands among the bloodiest single days in Israel's history.","Did you know the prophet Oded stopped an army from enslaving 200,000 fellow Israelites, and the captors then fed them, clothed the naked, and carried the feeble home to Jericho on donkeys?","Did you know Ahaz shut the doors of the house of the LORD and raised altars in every corner of Jerusalem, yet his own son Hezekiah would reopen those same doors?"], questions:[
{q:"How old was Ahaz when he began to reign, and how long did he reign in Jerusalem?", options:["20 years old, and he reigned 16 years","25 years old, and he reigned 29 years","16 years old, and he reigned 52 years","25 years old, and he reigned 16 years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:1",verse:"Ahaz was twenty years old when he began to reign, and he reigned sixteen years in Jerusalem: but he did not that which was right in the sight of the LORD, like David his father:", insight:"Ahaz began at twenty, younger than his father, but youth is no excuse for wickedness. The throne tested his heart, and his heart chose idols."},
{q:"In whose ways did Ahaz walk, unlike David his father?", options:["The kings of Israel","The kings of Assyria","The Canaanites","The Philistines"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:2",verse:"For he walked in the ways of the kings of Israel, and made also molten images for Baalim.", insight:"Ahaz copied the idolatrous northern kings instead of his godly forefathers. Whom we choose to imitate shapes whom we become."},
{q:"What did Ahaz make for Baalim?", options:["Molten images","Golden calves","Carved wooden idols","A brazen serpent"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:2",verse:"For he walked in the ways of the kings of Israel, and made also molten images for Baalim.", insight:"Ahaz poured his metal into gods of his own making. Idolatry always costs treasure and always returns nothing."},
{q:"In which valley did Ahaz burn incense and burn his children in the fire?", options:["The valley of the son of Hinnom","The valley of Jehoshaphat","The valley of Elah","The valley of Achor"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:3",verse:"Moreover he burnt incense in the valley of the son of Hinnom, and burnt his children in the fire, after the abominations of the heathen whom the LORD had cast out before the children of Israel.", insight:"Ahaz sacrificed his own children to idols, following the abominations of the heathen. Sin never stays private; it devours the most precious things first."},
{q:"Where did Ahaz sacrifice and burn incense?", options:["In the high places, on the hills, and under every green tree","In the temple courts only","On the Mount of Olives","At the brook Kidron"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:4",verse:"He sacrificed also and burnt incense in the high places, and on the hills, and under every green tree.", insight:"Ahaz worshipped everywhere except where God commanded. Worship in the wrong place, however sincere it feels, is still disobedience."},
{q:"Into whose hands did the LORD first deliver Ahaz?", options:["The king of Syria","The king of Assyria","Pharaoh of Egypt","The king of Moab"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:5",verse:"Wherefore the LORD his God delivered him into the hand of the king of Syria; and they smote him, and carried away a great multitude of them captives, and brought them to Damascus. And he was also delivered into the hand of the king of Israel, who smote him with a great slaughter.", insight:"Defeat by Syria was God's chastening hand, meant to turn Ahaz back. Trouble is often mercy wearing a stern face."},
{q:"How many valiant men of Judah did Pekah the son of Remaliah slay in one day?", options:["120,000","200,000","70,000","307,500"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:6",verse:"For Pekah the son of Remaliah slew in Judah an hundred and twenty thousand in one day, which were all valiant men; because they had forsaken the LORD God of their fathers.", insight:"One hundred and twenty thousand fell in a single day, a staggering loss. When a nation forsakes God, even its valiant men cannot stand."},
{q:"Why did Pekah prevail against Judah with such slaughter?", options:["Because they had forsaken the LORD God of their fathers","Because his army was larger","Because Judah had no walled cities","Because the Assyrians helped him"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:6",verse:"For Pekah the son of Remaliah slew in Judah an hundred and twenty thousand in one day, which were all valiant men; because they had forsaken the LORD God of their fathers.", insight:"The verse names the cause plainly: they had forsaken the LORD. Defeat is not always about numbers; it is about who fights for you."},
{q:"Whom did Zichri, a mighty man of Ephraim, slay in that battle?", options:["Maaseiah the king's son, Azrikam the governor of the house, and Elkanah next to the king","The high priest and two captains","Three sons of the prophet Oded","The princes of the Philistines"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:7",verse:"And Zichri, a mighty man of Ephraim, slew Maaseiah the king’s son, and Azrikam the governor of the house, and Elkanah that was next to the king.", insight:"The slaughter reached the palace itself: the king's son, his steward, and his closest aide. Judgment that begins at the top spares no rank."},
{q:"How many captives did the children of Israel carry away to Samaria?", options:["200,000 women, sons, and daughters","120,000 valiant men","100,000 children","70,000 elders"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:8",verse:"And the children of Israel carried away captive of their brethren two hundred thousand, women, sons, and daughters, and took also away much spoil from them, and brought the spoil to Samaria.", insight:"Two hundred thousand captives, mothers and children, were dragged toward slavery. Sin's harvest is always larger than the sowing."},
{q:"Which prophet went out to meet the host returning to Samaria?", options:["Oded","Isaiah","Amos","Micah"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:9",verse:"But a prophet of the LORD was there, whose name was Oded: and he went out before the host that came to Samaria, and said unto them, Behold, because the LORD God of your fathers was wroth with Judah, he hath delivered them into your hand, and ye have slain them in a rage that reacheth up unto heaven.", insight:"One prophet walked out alone to face a victorious army. God often sends a single courageous voice to stop a multitude headed the wrong way."},
{q:"What did Oded warn the Israelites they were intending to do?", options:["To keep the children of Judah as bondmen and bondwomen","To sacrifice the captives to Baal","To sell the captives to the Philistines","To make the captives build Samaria's walls"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:10",verse:"And now ye purpose to keep under the children of Judah and Jerusalem for bondmen and bondwomen unto you: but are there not with you, even with you, sins against the LORD your God?", insight:"Oded exposed their plan to enslave their own brethren and reminded them of their own sins. It is easy to judge another's trespass while planning your own."},
{q:"Which men stood up against bringing the captives into Samaria?", options:["Four heads of the children of Ephraim","The priests of Samaria","Pekah and his captains","The elders of Judah"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:12",verse:"Then certain of the heads of the children of Ephraim, Azariah the son of Johanan, Berechiah the son of Meshillemoth, and Jehizkiah the son of Shallum, and Amasa the son of Hadlai, stood up against them that came from the war,", insight:"Four leaders of Ephraim stood with the prophet against their own army. Righteousness sometimes requires opposing your own side."},
{q:"How did the captors treat the captives before sending them home?", options:["They clothed the naked, shod them, gave them food and drink, anointed them, and carried the feeble on asses","They armed them and sent them to fight","They made them swear loyalty to Samaria","They kept the spoil and released the people"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:15",verse:"And the men which were expressed by name rose up, and took the captives, and with the spoil clothed all that were naked among them, and arrayed them, and shod them, and gave them to eat and to drink, and anointed them, and carried all the feeble of them upon asses, and brought them to Jericho, the city of palm trees, to their brethren: then they returned to Samaria.", insight:"The same hands that took captives now fed, clothed, and carried the weak. True repentance restores what it took and then goes further in kindness."},
{q:"To which city were the captives brought and left with their brethren?", options:["Jericho, the city of palm trees","Samaria","Bethel","Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:15",verse:"And the men which were expressed by name rose up, and took the captives, and with the spoil clothed all that were naked among them, and arrayed them, and shod them, and gave them to eat and to drink, and anointed them, and carried all the feeble of them upon asses, and brought them to Jericho, the city of palm trees, to their brethren: then they returned to Samaria.", insight:"They carried the feeble all the way to Jericho, the city of palm trees. Mercy that stops halfway is not mercy; love finishes the journey."},
{q:"To whom did Ahaz send for help in his distress?", options:["The kings of Assyria","The king of Egypt","The Philistines","The Edomites"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:16",verse:"At that time did king Ahaz send unto the kings of Assyria to help him.", insight:"Instead of turning to the LORD, Ahaz hired Assyria. Running to the wrong helper in trouble only trades one bondage for another."},
{q:"Which king of Assyria came to Ahaz, distressed him, and strengthened him not?", options:["Tilgathpilneser","Shalmaneser","Sennacherib","Pul"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:20",verse:"And Tilgath-pilneser king of Assyria came unto him, and distressed him, but strengthened him not.", insight:"The ally Ahaz paid with temple treasures only added to his distress. Help bought at the price of faithfulness always disappoints."},
{q:"To whose gods did Ahaz sacrifice, thinking they would help him?", options:["The gods of Damascus, the gods of the kings of Syria","The gods of Assyria","Baal and Ashtaroth","The gods of the Philistines"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:23",verse:"For he sacrificed unto the gods of Damascus, which smote him: and he said, Because the gods of the kings of Syria help them, therefore will I sacrifice to them, that they may help me. But they were the ruin of him, and of all Israel.", insight:"Ahaz worshipped the gods of the very nation that defeated him, reasoning they must be strong. Defeat never proves the enemy's gods; it proves our need of our own God."},
{q:"What did Ahaz do to the house of God?", options:["He gathered its vessels, cut them in pieces, shut up the doors, and made altars in every corner of Jerusalem","He burned it with fire","He turned it into a palace","He gave it to the Assyrians"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:24",verse:"And Ahaz gathered together the vessels of the house of God, and cut in pieces the vessels of the house of God, and shut up the doors of the house of the LORD, and he made him altars in every corner of Jerusalem.", insight:"Ahaz smashed the temple vessels and barred its doors, then filled the city with altars. A heart closed to God always finds somewhere else to worship."},
{q:"Where was Ahaz buried when he died?", options:["In the city, but not in the sepulchres of the kings","In the sepulchres of the kings with honor","In the valley of Hinnom","In Samaria"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 28:27",verse:"And Ahaz slept with his fathers, and they buried him in the city, even in Jerusalem: but they brought him not into the sepulchres of the kings of Israel: and Hezekiah his son reigned in his stead.", insight:"Ahaz was buried in Jerusalem but denied the kings' sepulchres. A lifetime of choices writes its own epitaph, and some honors cannot be bought back."}
]},
{n:29, name:"Hezekiah Cleanses the Temple", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 29:10",verse:"Now it is in mine heart to make a covenant with the LORD God of Israel, that his fierce wrath may turn away from us."}, facts:["Did you know Hezekiah reopened the doors of the house of the LORD in the first month of his first year, making the restoration of God's house his very first act as king?","Did you know it took the priests and Levites sixteen days to cleanse the temple, carrying all the uncleanness out to the brook Kidron?","Did you know the priests were too few to flay all the burnt offerings, so the Levites, who were more upright in heart to sanctify themselves, stepped in to help them?"], questions:[
{q:"How old was Hezekiah when he began to reign, and how long did he reign in Jerusalem?", options:["25 years old, and he reigned 29 years","20 years old, and he reigned 16 years","25 years old, and he reigned 16 years","16 years old, and he reigned 52 years"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:1",verse:"Hezekiah began to reign when he was five and twenty years old, and he reigned nine and twenty years in Jerusalem. And his mother’s name was Abijah, the daughter of Zechariah.", insight:"Hezekiah began at twenty five, the same age as his grandfather Jotham, but his reign ran twenty nine years. God gave him time, and he spent it on restoration."},
{q:"Who was Hezekiah's mother?", options:["Abijah, the daughter of Zechariah","Jerushah, the daughter of Zadok","Jecoliah of Jerusalem","Hephzibah"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:1",verse:"Hezekiah began to reign when he was five and twenty years old, and he reigned nine and twenty years in Jerusalem. And his mother’s name was Abijah, the daughter of Zechariah.", insight:"Hezekiah's mother Abijah came from a godly line, yet his father Ahaz was wicked. Grace can raise a reformer in the very house of apostasy."},
{q:"When did Hezekiah open the doors of the house of the LORD and repair them?", options:["In the first year of his reign, in the first month","In the third year, in the seventh month","In the first year, in the seventh month","In the seventh year, in the first month"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:3",verse:"He in the first year of his reign, in the first month, opened the doors of the house of the LORD, and repaired them.", insight:"The first month of the first year: Hezekiah wasted no time on God's house. First things belong first, and delay is its own kind of disobedience."},
{q:"Where did Hezekiah gather the priests and the Levites?", options:["Into the east street","Into the porch of the temple","Into the inner court","Into the upper chamber"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:4",verse:"And he brought in the priests and the Levites, and gathered them together into the east street,", insight:"Hezekiah called the ministers together in the open street before giving them their charge. Reform begins with gathering the workers and speaking plainly."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah command the Levites to carry forth out of the sanctuary?", options:["The filthiness","The old vessels","The lamps","The shewbread"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:5",verse:"And said unto them, Hear me, ye Levites, sanctify now yourselves, and sanctify the house of the LORD God of your fathers, and carry forth the filthiness out of the holy place.", insight:"Before worship could resume, the filthiness had to be carried out. Cleansing always precedes consecration; God will not be worshipped in a cluttered house."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah say their fathers had done?", options:["Trespassed, done evil, forsaken the LORD, and turned away their faces","Kept the law but neglected mercy","Worshipped in ignorance","Served the LORD but loved idols too"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:6",verse:"For our fathers have trespassed, and done that which was evil in the eyes of the LORD our God, and have forsaken him, and have turned away their faces from the habitation of the LORD, and turned their backs.", insight:"Hezekiah named the sin honestly: trespass, evil, forsaking, turned faces. Revival starts when we stop softening the description of what went wrong."},
{q:"What had the fathers done to the house of God?", options:["Shut up the doors of the porch, put out the lamps, and burned no incense","Torn down the walls","Sold the vessels to Assyria","Set up idols in the holy place"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:7",verse:"Also they have shut up the doors of the porch, and put out the lamps, and have not burned incense nor offered burnt offerings in the holy place unto the God of Israel.", insight:"They shut the doors, quenched the lamps, and stopped the incense: worship did not die in a day, it faded step by step. Neglect is a slow door closing."},
{q:"What was in Hezekiah's heart to do with the LORD God of Israel?", options:["To make a covenant with Him","To build Him a new temple","To carry the ark to Jerusalem","To number the people"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:10",verse:"Now it is in mine heart to make a covenant with the LORD God of Israel, that his fierce wrath may turn away from us.", insight:"Hezekiah's reform began as a resolve of the heart: a covenant with God. Lasting change starts inside before it shows outside."},
{q:"For what did Hezekiah say the LORD had chosen the Levites?", options:["To stand before Him, to serve Him, to minister, and to burn incense","To judge the people and collect tithes","To teach the law in the cities","To guard the temple gates only"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:11",verse:"My sons, be not now negligent: for the LORD hath chosen you to stand before him, to serve him, and that ye should minister unto him, and burn incense.", insight:"The Levites were chosen to stand before God and serve, a holy calling, not a job. Remembering we are chosen for His presence dignifies the work."},
{q:"Which Levites arose first to sanctify the house, of the sons of the Kohathites?", options:["Mahath the son of Amasai, and Joel the son of Azariah","Kish the son of Abdi, and Azariah the son of Jehalelel","Joah the son of Zimmah, and Eden the son of Joah","Shimri and Jeiel, the sons of Elizaphan"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:12",verse:"Then the Levites arose, Mahath the son of Amasai, and Joel the son of Azariah, of the sons of the Kohathites: and of the sons of Merari, Kish the son of Abdi, and Azariah the son of Jehalelel: and of the Gershonites; Joah the son of Zimmah, and Eden the son of Joah:", insight:"The record names the men who rose first, Mahath and Joel among them. God remembers the names of those who answer the call to cleanse His house."},
{q:"Where did the Levites carry the uncleanness they brought out of the temple?", options:["To the brook Kidron","To the valley of Hinnom","To the dung gate","To the wilderness"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:16",verse:"And the priests went into the inner part of the house of the LORD, to cleanse it, and brought out all the uncleanness that they found in the temple of the LORD into the court of the house of the LORD. And the Levites took it, to carry it out abroad into the brook Kidron.", insight:"The filth was carried all the way out to the Kidron, not piled in a corner. Dealing with sin means removing it completely, not rearranging it."},
{q:"How long did the cleansing of the temple take?", options:["Sixteen days, from the first day to the sixteenth day of the first month","Eight days","Seven days","Thirty days"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:17",verse:"Now they began on the first day of the first month to sanctify, and on the eighth day of the month came they to the porch of the LORD: so they sanctified the house of the LORD in eight days; and in the sixteenth day of the first month they made an end.", insight:"Sixteen days of steady, thorough work cleansed what years of neglect had defiled. Restoration takes time, and patient labor is not wasted time."},
{q:"What did they bring for a sin offering for the kingdom, the sanctuary, and Judah?", options:["Seven bullocks, seven rams, seven lambs, and seven he goats","Seventy bullocks, one hundred rams, and two hundred lambs","One bullock and one goat","Twelve bullocks for the twelve tribes"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:21",verse:"And they brought seven bullocks, and seven rams, and seven lambs, and seven he goats, for a sin offering for the kingdom, and for the sanctuary, and for Judah. And he commanded the priests the sons of Aaron to offer them on the altar of the LORD.", insight:"Seven of each, the number of completeness, for kingdom, sanctuary, and people. Atonement had to cover every part of the nation's life, none left out."},
{q:"What did the king and the congregation do with the he goats of the sin offering?", options:["They laid their hands upon them, and the priests killed them to make reconciliation","They set them free in the wilderness","They gave them to the Levites","They burned them without the camp"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:23",verse:"And they brought forth the he goats for the sin offering before the king and the congregation; and they laid their hands upon them:", insight:"Laying hands on the offering meant owning the sin it carried. Forgiveness becomes real when we personally place our guilt on the substitute God provides."},
{q:"With what instruments did Hezekiah set the Levites in the house of the LORD?", options:["Cymbals, psalteries, and harps","Trumpets and cornets","Pipes and tabrets","Harps and organs only"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:25",verse:"And he set the Levites in the house of the LORD with cymbals, with psalteries, and with harps, according to the commandment of David, and of Gad the king’s seer, and Nathan the prophet: for so was the commandment of the LORD by his prophets.", insight:"Cymbals, psalteries, and harps filled the cleansed house with ordered music. Cleansed hearts make music; joy returns where holiness is restored."},
{q:"According to whose commandment were the Levites set in their order?", options:["David, Gad the king's seer, and Nathan the prophet","Moses and Aaron","Samuel and David","David only"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:25",verse:"And he set the Levites in the house of the LORD with cymbals, with psalteries, and with harps, according to the commandment of David, and of Gad the king’s seer, and Nathan the prophet: for so was the commandment of the LORD by his prophets.", insight:"Hezekiah did not invent the worship order; he restored David's, confirmed by Gad and Nathan. True reform returns to God's appointed pattern, it does not improvise."},
{q:"When did the song of the LORD begin?", options:["When the burnt offering began","At sunrise","When the priests entered","After the sacrifices ended"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:27",verse:"And Hezekiah commanded to offer the burnt offering upon the altar. And when the burnt offering began, the song of the LORD began also with the trumpets, and with the instruments ordained by David king of Israel.", insight:"Song and sacrifice rose together; worship and atonement belong side by side. Praise is sweetest when it stands on the ground of a cleansed conscience."},
{q:"How many animals did the congregation bring for the burnt offering?", options:["70 bullocks, 100 rams, and 200 lambs","600 oxen and 3,000 sheep","1,000 bullocks and 7,000 sheep","7 bullocks, 7 rams, 7 lambs, and 7 goats"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:32",verse:"And the number of the burnt offerings, which the congregation brought, was threescore and ten bullocks, an hundred rams, and two hundred lambs: all these were for a burnt offering to the LORD.", insight:"Seventy bullocks, a hundred rams, two hundred lambs: the people gave generously once their hearts were stirred. A revived people are a giving people."},
{q:"What were the consecrated things the people brought?", options:["600 oxen and 3,000 sheep","70 bullocks and 100 rams","1,000 bullocks and 10,000 sheep","Drink offerings and meal offerings"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:33",verse:"And the consecrated things were six hundred oxen and three thousand sheep.", insight:"Six hundred oxen and three thousand sheep were set apart as holy. Consecration means marking something as God's own, no longer common."},
{q:"Why did Hezekiah and all the people rejoice at the end?", options:["Because God had prepared the people, for the thing was done suddenly","Because the temple was more glorious than Solomon's","Because the Assyrians had departed","Because the priests were numerous"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 29:36",verse:"And Hezekiah rejoiced, and all the people, that God had prepared the people: for the thing was done suddenly.", insight:"The work was done suddenly because God had prepared the people. When hearts are ready, God can do in days what years of effort could not."}
]},
{n:30, name:"The Great Passover", seal:{ref:"2 Chronicles 30:20",verse:"And the LORD hearkened to Hezekiah, and healed the people."}, facts:["Did you know Hezekiah kept the Passover in the second month? The priests had not sanctified themselves in time for the first, and the law itself allowed a second month Passover.","Did you know Hezekiah's letters invited the northern tribes to return to the LORD? Many mocked the messengers, but some from Asher, Manasseh, and Zebulun humbled themselves and came.","Did you know the joy in Jerusalem at Hezekiah's Passover had not been seen since the days of Solomon?"], questions:[
{q:"To whom did Hezekiah send letters inviting them to keep the Passover at Jerusalem?", options:["To all Israel and Judah, and to Ephraim and Manasseh","To Judah only","To the priests and Levites","To the kings round about"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:1",verse:"And Hezekiah sent to all Israel and Judah, and wrote letters also to Ephraim and Manasseh, that they should come to the house of the LORD at Jerusalem, to keep the passover unto the LORD God of Israel.", insight:"Hezekiah invited the whole nation, north and south, to one Passover. God's table is wide, and unity begins with an open invitation."},
{q:"In which month did Hezekiah and the congregation agree to keep the Passover?", options:["The second month","The first month","The third month","The seventh month"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:2",verse:"For the king had taken counsel, and his princes, and all the congregation in Jerusalem, to keep the passover in the second month.", insight:"They kept it in the second month because the first had been missed. God makes room for the willing latecomer; better a second month Passover than none at all."},
{q:"Why could they not keep the Passover at the appointed time?", options:["The priests had not sanctified themselves sufficiently, and the people were not gathered","The temple doors were still shut","The Assyrians threatened Jerusalem","There were no lambs to be found"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:3",verse:"For they could not keep it at that time, because the priests had not sanctified themselves sufficiently, neither had the people gathered themselves together to Jerusalem.", insight:"The priests were not ready and the people were not gathered, so they waited rather than rush unprepared. Honest delay for readiness is better than hasty irreverence."},
{q:"From where to where did Hezekiah's proclamation go out?", options:["From Beersheba to Dan","From Dan to Beersheba","From Jerusalem to Samaria","From Gilgal to Bethel"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:5",verse:"So they established a decree to make proclamation throughout all Israel, from Beer-sheba even to Dan, that they should come to keep the passover unto the LORD God of Israel at Jerusalem: for they had not done it of a long time in such sort as it was written.", insight:"The call ran from Beersheba to Dan, the whole length of the land. When God moves, the invitation should travel as far as our voice can reach."},
{q:"What was the burden of Hezekiah's letters to the people?", options:["Turn again unto the LORD God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel","Come and see the cleansed temple","Bring your tithes to Jerusalem","Prepare for war with Assyria"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:6",verse:"So the posts went with the letters from the king and his princes throughout all Israel and Judah, and according to the commandment of the king, saying, Ye children of Israel, turn again unto the LORD God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel, and he will return to the remnant of you, that are escaped out of the hand of the kings of Assyria.", insight:"The message was repentance: turn again to the God of the fathers. Every true invitation from God is, at its heart, a call to come home."},
{q:"What did the letters urge the people not to be?", options:["Stiffnecked, but to yield themselves unto the LORD and enter His sanctuary","Fearful of the Assyrians","Slow to bring their offerings","Proud of their heritage"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:8",verse:"Now be ye not stiffnecked, as your fathers were, but yield yourselves unto the LORD, and enter into his sanctuary, which he hath sanctified for ever: and serve the LORD your God, that the fierceness of his wrath may turn away from you.", insight:"They were warned not to be stiffnecked like their fathers, but to yield. A stiff neck cannot bow in worship; yielding is the first movement of return."},
{q:"What promise did the letters give to those who turned again to the LORD?", options:["He is gracious and merciful, and will not turn away His face from them","He would make them rulers over nations","He would give them the land of their enemies","He would send rain in due season"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:9",verse:"For if ye turn again unto the LORD, your brethren and your children shall find compassion before them that lead them captive, so that they shall come again into this land: for the LORD your God is gracious and merciful, and will not turn away his face from you, if ye return unto him.", insight:"The promise was God's own character: gracious and merciful, His face not turned away. We return not to a bargain but to a Father's welcome."},
{q:"How did many in Ephraim, Manasseh, and Zebulun treat the messengers?", options:["They laughed them to scorn and mocked them","They received them gladly","They imprisoned them","They ignored them completely"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:10",verse:"So the posts passed from city to city through the country of Ephraim and Manasseh even unto Zebulun: but they laughed them to scorn, and mocked them.", insight:"The messengers were mocked as they carried God's invitation. Faithful witnesses are sometimes laughed at, but the message must still be delivered."},
{q:"Who humbled themselves and came to Jerusalem for the Passover?", options:["Divers of Asher, Manasseh, and Zebulun","All the tribe of Ephraim","The priests of Bethel","The elders of Samaria"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:11",verse:"Nevertheless divers of Asher and Manasseh and of Zebulun humbled themselves, and came to Jerusalem.", insight:"Some from Asher, Manasseh, and Zebulun humbled themselves and came. It only takes a humble few to answer, and their coming encourages all the rest."},
{q:"What did the hand of God give to the people of Judah?", options:["One heart to do the commandment of the king and the princes","Great riches","Victory over their enemies","A new covenant"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:12",verse:"Also in Judah the hand of God was to give them one heart to do the commandment of the king and of the princes, by the word of the LORD.", insight:"God gave them one heart to obey together. Unity is not manufactured by planning; it is given by the hand of God to willing people."},
{q:"What did the people do with the altars and the altars for incense in Jerusalem?", options:["They took them away and cast them into the brook Kidron","They burned them in the temple court","They broke them and sold the metal","They left them standing"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:14",verse:"And they arose and took away the altars that were in Jerusalem, and all the altars for incense took they away, and cast them into the brook Kidron.", insight:"Before the feast, the idol altars were torn down and thrown into the Kidron. You cannot feast with God while keeping altars to idols; cleansing comes before celebrating."},
{q:"On what day did they kill the Passover?", options:["The fourteenth day of the second month","The fourteenth day of the first month","The tenth day of the second month","The first day of unleavened bread"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:15",verse:"Then they killed the passover on the fourteenth day of the second month: and the priests and the Levites were ashamed, and sanctified themselves, and brought in the burnt offerings into the house of the LORD.", insight:"They kept the fourteenth day, only in the second month. Obedience kept late is still obedience when the heart is right."},
{q:"Why did the Levites have charge of killing the Passover lambs for many in the congregation?", options:["Because many were not sanctified","Because the priests were too few","Because the people were uncircumcised","Because it was the second month"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:17",verse:"For there were many in the congregation that were not sanctified: therefore the Levites had the charge of the killing of the passovers for every one that was not clean, to sanctify them unto the LORD.", insight:"Many were not yet cleansed, so the Levites served in their place. God's servants step in to help the unready, not to shame them."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah pray for those who ate the Passover otherwise than it was written?", options:["The good LORD pardon every one","The LORD cleanse them with hyssop","The LORD teach them the law","The LORD accept their offering"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:18",verse:"For a multitude of the people, even many of Ephraim, and Manasseh, Issachar, and Zebulun, had not cleansed themselves, yet did they eat the passover otherwise than it was written. But Hezekiah prayed for them, saying, The good LORD pardon every one", insight:"Hezekiah prayed, The good LORD pardon every one. Intercession covers the gap between sincere hearts and imperfect observance."},
{q:"Whom did Hezekiah ask the LORD to pardon?", options:["Every one that prepareth his heart to seek God, though not cleansed according to the purification of the sanctuary","The priests who were not sanctified","The strangers from the north","The children who ate unknowingly"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:19",verse:"That prepareth his heart to seek God, the LORD God of his fathers, though he be not cleansed according to the purification of the sanctuary.", insight:"The pardon was for those whose hearts were prepared to seek God. God looks first at the direction of the heart, then at the details of the hands."},
{q:"How did the LORD answer Hezekiah's prayer?", options:["He hearkened to Hezekiah, and healed the people","He sent fire upon the altar","He struck the unclean with leprosy","He commanded them to keep another Passover"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:20",verse:"And the LORD hearkened to Hezekiah, and healed the people.", insight:"The LORD heard and healed the people. Prayer for others moves God's hand; intercession is never wasted."},
{q:"How long did they keep the feast of unleavened bread, and in what spirit?", options:["Seven days with great gladness","Fourteen days with solemnity","Seven days with fasting","Three days with great gladness"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:21",verse:"And the children of Israel that were present at Jerusalem kept the feast of unleavened bread seven days with great gladness: and the Levites and the priests praised the LORD day by day, singing with loud instruments unto the LORD.", insight:"Seven days of great gladness, with daily praise. Joy sustained over days, not just felt in a moment, marks a true work of God."},
{q:"What did the whole assembly take counsel to do after the seven days?", options:["To keep another seven days","To return to their homes","To build new altars","To number the people"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:23",verse:"And the whole assembly took counsel to keep other seven days: and they kept other seven days with gladness.", insight:"They chose to keep another seven days, unwilling for the joy to end. When God's presence is sweet, His people linger."},
{q:"What did Hezekiah and the princes give for the sacrifices?", options:["Hezekiah gave 1,000 bullocks and 7,000 sheep; the princes gave 1,000 bullocks and 10,000 sheep","Hezekiah gave 70 bullocks and the princes gave 100 rams","Each gave 600 oxen and 3,000 sheep","Hezekiah gave 7 of every kind"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:24",verse:"For Hezekiah king of Judah did give to the congregation a thousand bullocks and seven thousand sheep; and the princes gave to the congregation a thousand bullocks and ten thousand sheep: and a great number of priests sanctified themselves.", insight:"King and princes gave lavishly: thousands of animals for the people's offerings. Generous leaders feed the worship they call others to."},
{q:"Where did the prayer of the priests and Levites go up to?", options:["To His holy dwelling place, even unto heaven","To the mercy seat","To the Mount of Olives","To the courts of the temple"], a:0, ref:"2 Chronicles 30:27",verse:"Then the priests the Levites arose and blessed the people: and their voice was heard, and their prayer came up to his holy dwelling place, even unto heaven.", insight:"Their blessing rose to God's holy dwelling, even unto heaven. Prayer offered on earth is heard in heaven; no distance diminishes it."}
]}
]);
